---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import MdToHtml from '../../components/MdToHtml.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Markdown to HTML Converter" description="Convert Markdown (headers, bold, italic, links, lists) into clean HTML.">
  <MdToHtml client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why converting matters beyond just viewing rendered Markdown</h2>
  <p>Many platforms (email templates, older CMS systems, some documentation tools) accept HTML but not Markdown directly — converting lets you write in the faster, more readable Markdown syntax and paste the resulting HTML wherever it's actually needed.</p>

  <h2>How to use this converter</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste your Markdown content.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated HTML output.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>Markdown <code>**bold** and *italic*</code> converts to <code>&lt;strong&gt;bold&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;em&gt;italic&lt;/em&gt;</code>.</p>

  <h2>Common Markdown Conversion Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Expecting support for every Markdown extension.</strong> This handles core syntax (headers, bold/italic, links, simple lists) — tables, nested lists, and other GitHub Flavored Markdown extensions may need a more complete parser.</li>
    <li><strong>Not sanitizing the output</strong> if user-generated Markdown is being converted and rendered elsewhere — always sanitize HTML output before rendering untrusted content.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Markdown to HTML Converter</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Converting a Markdown-written email into HTML for an email client</li>
    <li>Preparing Markdown content for a platform that only accepts HTML</li>
    <li>Quickly previewing what a piece of Markdown will render as in HTML</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Markdown to HTML FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this support tables and nested lists?</strong> No — it handles core Markdown syntax; more complex structures need a fuller Markdown parser/library.</p>
</ToolLayout>
